nifmake builds with nimony (and still with classic Nim), so the self-hosted lane can run it on Temen.

Why: nimony's build driver (`nimony c` / `check`) hands the dependency graph to nifmake, and the
self-hosted lane (#1609) runs every phase as a Temen module compiled by nimony with no C. Upstream
nifmake is a classic-Nim-only program: it uses stdlib conveniences nimony's stdlib does not have
(`setLastModificationTime`, `tables.withValue`, `align`/`alignLeft`, `getCurrentProcessId`,
`moveFile`/`removeFile`, a one-element `seq.insert`, `wait4` with a `rusage`, `foldl`, int `/`
yielding a float, multi-argument `writeLine`, `except ValueError`/`OSError`, a capturing `sort`
comparator, a nil `ptr`, raising table and OS calls outside `try`). It also fixes a latent bug:
`countProcessors()` is 0 when the count cannot be detected, and `-j` then scheduled nothing and
reported the unbuilt graph as a success. Not fixed upstream as of nim-lang/nimony master 6ca46f17 (2026-09-30).

What: every change is either `when defined(nimony)` or shared code valid under both compilers,
using upstream's own dual-compile idioms (`lib/compat2.nim`'s `onRaiseQuit` / `getOrQuit`).
Behaviour is unchanged, except that a nimony-built nifmake's `--profile` reports no peak memory
(nimony's `posix` binds `wait4` without a `rusage`). The patched file builds with classic Nim (the
pinned devel) and with nimony, and either binary drives `nimony c` to the same result.

Applied to a COPY of the pinned sources at build time (`scripts/nim-toolchain.sh`); the
vendored `nimony/` submodule is never modified. Against the submodule pin (6ca46f17).
Drop it when upstream nifmake builds with nimony.
